  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 259 AM EST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\n- Drying out and gradual warming through Tuesday\n\nLast vestiges of the upper low and lake effect rain showers have \nshifted just east of the forecast area this morning. These showers \nare on the far western edge of the upper low, and are associated \nwith higher level moisture with the occluded low. Plenty of low \nlevel moisture remains across the forecast area this morning. We \nwill see this tend to move out/mix out as the upper low continues to \nrotate east.\n\nWe remain on the anti-cyclonic side of the upper level jet from \ntoday right on through Tuesday. This will limit the stratocumulus \nclouds and rain chances overall. We do remain close enough to the \nnorthern branch of the upper jet that short waves embedded in that \nflow and associated sfc fronts will brush the area. A couple of \nthese fronts move through early and then late Sunday. There is no \nappreciable moisture with these fronts for our area, as the source \nregion will be over the mid and upper Plains. \n\nHigh pressure then builds in slowly for Monday, and sticks around \nthrough Tuesday. 850 mb temperatures rise to as warm as around 8C on \nTuesday. These temperatures aloft will support max sfc temps well \nabove average at least in the upper 50s to lower 60s. \n\n- Next chance of rain Wednesday with another cool down to follow\n\nThings become a bit unsettled around Wednesday and are likely to \nremain unsettled through next Friday. As the upper ridge axis moves \neast of the forecast area by Wednesday, we are likely to see two \ndecent pieces of energy move toward the area and likely consolidate \ninto one larger upper low.\n\nWe will see one wave come eastward out of the mid-Plains. Another \nstronger upper wave dives SE from Canada around the same time. The \nmodels have been showing this occurring beginning around the \nWednesday time frame. The details are likely a little uncertain as \nis the case anytime you have phasing systems as exact timing of each \none will dictate the time of phasing.\n\nGenerally speaking, rain chances will arrive on Wednesday in advance \nof the upper waves. Much of the synoptic precipitation shifts east \nby Thursday as the new upper low induces cyclogenesis over the East \nCoast.\n\nWhat we see here is colder air dropping in for Thursday and Friday. \nThis is likely to produce some diurnal showers, and lake effect as \n850 mb temperatures drop to around -2 to -3C, allowing for \nsufficient instability in the lower levels, and deep instability \nunder the cold pool aloft. Max temps are likely to cool off to just \nthe 40s late in the week once again like the current pattern.",
